This document will walk you through the instructions to setup a static IP on the device. All of these steps are followed
in the current OS of the pi. If you need to rebuild your own pi with the identical settings, follow the steps shown below.
The current pi uses Ubuntu 22.04 server, and the steps have been completed using a linux machine connected via ssh.
To setup your own static IP, you will need to create your own netplan with the correct IP. Let's first take a look at the
netplan that exists on your pi (right after flashing on first boot).

## Find and see your current netplan ##
command>    ls /etc/netplan
output>     50-cloud-init.yaml

command>    cat /etc/netplan/50-cloud-init.yaml
output>     # This file is generated from information provided by the datasource.  Changes
      >     # to it will not persist across an instance reboot.  To disable cloud-init's
      >     # network configuration capabilities, write a file
      >     # /etc/cloud/cloud.cfg.d/99-disable-network-config.cfg with the following:
      >     # network: {config: disabled}
      >     network:
      >         ethernets:
      >             eth0:
      >                 dhcp4: true
      >                 optional: true
      >         version: 2

You can see that by default dhcp4 is set to true. This means that the pi will always contact the DHCP server (router or
access point running the closest DHCP server usualy) and dynamically asign an IP to the pi. This is not ideal.       



>           eth0:
       >               dhcp4: false
       >               dhcp6: false
       >               optional: true
       >               addresses:
       >                   - 192.168.165.10/24

We will use the IP address 192.168.165.10/24 as it is not used by any of our other devices. This IP may not work for you
so adjust it accordingly. Keep in mind, this is the static IP. Additionally `networkd` is the rendering service. `dhcp4`
and `dhcp6` are set to flase so the dynamic IP issue will not occur. Also the optional flag has been applied to prevent
getting stuck on boot. 

NOTE: Internet access will require wifi. We use wifi for any internet related tasks such as downloading packages.

## Internet access through wifi using cli (always on reboot) ##
command>    sudo apt update
command>    sudo apt upgrade
command>    sudo apt install network-manager

command>    nmcli device wifi list
command>    nmcli device wifi connect <your SSID> password <wifi password>
